
# Milestone 3: Econometric Models

## Project
- Team: Lailah, Stevie, Isabelle, Sienna
- Research question: How do U.S. REIT performance metrics vary with changes in U.S. 15-year mortgage rates over time?
- Final analysis file: data/final/reit_fred_analysis_panel.csv

## 1) Model Goal and Data Design

### Core idea
- Estimate whether annual U.S. 15-year mortgage-rate changes are associated with changes in REIT performance.
- Use the M1-cleaned and M2-validated panel structure so the model is consistent with the project’s earlier data pipeline and exploratory findings.
- Treat the mortgage-rate series as a national annual macro variable and model its association with REIT outcomes using firm fixed effects.

### Final panel used in M3
- Observations: 2,965 rows × 26 columns in the full analysis file
- Modeling sample: 2,428 complete cases for the returns model and 2,429 complete cases for the log market equity model
- Time span: 2000–2023
- Entities: 286 REITs

### Why this specification
- Full year fixed effects would absorb the national mortgage-rate regressor, so the model uses entity fixed effects plus a year trend and crisis-period controls.
- This preserves identification from time variation while still controlling for long-run market drift and crisis shocks.

## 2) Baseline Model A Results

### Main fixed-effects specification
- Outcome 1: annual REIT return average
- Outcome 2: log average market equity
- Key regressor: U.S. 15-year mortgage rate, lagged 2 years
- Controls: average assets, debt/assets, cash/assets, ROE, book-to-market, beta, year trend, and crisis indicators
- Estimation: panel fixed effects with clustered standard errors by entity and year

### Table A. Main regression results

| Variable | REIT return model | Log market equity model |
|---|---:|---:|
| Mortgage rate lag 2 | -0.0007 (0.0050) | -0.1141*** (0.0370) |
| Year trend | -0.0006 (0.0015) | 0.0573*** (0.0113) |
| Average assets | -0.0000 (0.0000) | 0.0001*** (0.0000) |
| Debt / assets | -0.0050 (0.0098) | -1.4903*** (0.3793) |
| Cash / assets | -0.0291 (0.0184) | -1.4567*** (0.3695) |
| ROE | -0.0004 (0.0005) | -0.0053 (0.0134) |
| Book-to-market | 0.0053 (0.0059) | -0.4892*** (0.0728) |
| Beta | 0.0136*** (0.0049) | -0.2414*** (0.0645) |
| 2008-2009 crisis | -0.0081 (0.0235) | 0.0620 (0.0858) |
| 2020-2021 crisis | 0.0064 (0.0157) | 0.0270 (0.0527) |
| 2022-2023 tightening | -0.0134 (0.0128) | -0.2102** (0.0997) |
| Entity FE | Yes | Yes |
| Time FE | No, year trend + crisis controls | No, year trend + crisis controls |
| Clustered SE | Yes, entity + year | Yes, entity + year |
| N | 2,428 | 2,429 |
| Within R2 | 0.075 | 0.689 |

### Interpretation
- The mortgage-rate coefficient is not statistically significant in the return model.
- The mortgage-rate coefficient is negative and statistically significant in the market-equity model.
- That pattern suggests mortgage-rate variation is reflected more clearly in valuation levels than in annual return averages.

## 3) Diagnostics

### Heteroskedasticity test
- Breusch-Pagan LM stat: 253.4361
- Breusch-Pagan LM p-value: 5.3278e-48
- Breusch-Pagan F stat: 25.5977
- Breusch-Pagan F p-value: 8.2767e-51

### Multicollinearity check
- Maximum VIF: 11.84 for debt/assets
- Mortgage rate lag 2 VIF: 8.05
- Year trend VIF: 8.35
- These values are consistent with a macro panel where the national rate series co-moves with broad time trends.

### Diagnostic figures
- Residuals vs fitted: results/figures/M3_residuals_vs_fitted.png
